{:edb/db-before-t 4914 :edb/db-after-t 4915 :edb/tx-data [[13194139538227 50 #inst "2026-10-20T01:08:43.351Z" 13194139538227 17592186576411 true] [17592186314780 1074 "We can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{3-2x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({5}^{3}\\cdot {5}^{-2x}) \\\\  & ={5}^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{-2x} \\\\  & =125\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{({5}^{-2})}^{x} \\\\  & =125\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{{5}^{2}})}^{x} \\\\  & =125\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{25})}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{25})}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/37/3795890dac6f542c8aa75d61deb8a03192a41532270b74eafcd972e82ac6721e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function decreases to zero as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{25})}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{3-2x}=125\\cdot 0=0.\n$$" 13194139538227 17592186576411 false] [17592186314780 1074 "We can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{3-2x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({5}^{3}\\cdot {5}^{-2x}) \\\\  & ={5}^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{-2x} \\\\  & =125\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{({5}^{-2})}^{x} \\\\  & =125\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{{5}^{2}}\\right)}^{x} \\\\  & =125\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{25}\\right)}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{25}\\right)}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/37/3795890dac6f542c8aa75d61deb8a03192a41532270b74eafcd972e82ac6721e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function decreases to zero as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{25}\\right)}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{3-2x}=125\\cdot 0=0.\n$$" 13194139538227 17592186576411 true] [17592186314781 1074 "We can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {3}^{2-3x}) & =(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{3}^{2-3x} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({3}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{3}^{-3x} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 9\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{({3}^{-3})}^{x} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{9}{2})\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{{3}^{3}})}^{x} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{9}{2})\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{27})}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{27})}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/60/60c4ef99a2617fd076e6350ba68e14311ebc41b9b0f33fcc07b871198dfc103d.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{27})}^{x}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{9}{2})\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{27})}^{x}=(-\\frac{9}{2})\\cdot \\infty =-\\infty .\n$$" 13194139538227 17592186576411 false] [17592186314781 1074 "We can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {3}^{2-3x}) & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{3}^{2-3x} \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({3}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{3}^{-3x} \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot 9\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{({3}^{-3})}^{x} \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{9}{2}\\right)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{{3}^{3}}\\right)}^{x} \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{9}{2}\\right)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{27}\\right)}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{27}\\right)}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/60/60c4ef99a2617fd076e6350ba68e14311ebc41b9b0f33fcc07b871198dfc103d.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{27}\\right)}^{x}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\frac{9}{2}\\right)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{27}\\right)}^{x}=\\left(-\\frac{9}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\infty =-\\infty .\n$$" 13194139538227 17592186576411 true] [17592186314782 1074 "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{6}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2}{4{x}^{6}-5x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{6}})}{(\\frac{4{x}^{6}}{{x}^{6}}-\\frac{5x}{{x}^{6}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{6}})}{(4-\\frac{5}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{4-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538227 17592186576411 false] [17592186314782 1074 "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{6}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2}{4{x}^{6}-5x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{6}}\\right)}{(\\frac{4{x}^{6}}{{x}^{6}}-\\frac{5x}{{x}^{6}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{6}}\\right)}{(4-\\frac{5}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{4-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538227 17592186576411 true] [17592186314785 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A.$\n\nThe solution is $y=A\\,\\cdot $\n{{field-1}}" 13194139538227 17592186576411 false] [17592186314785 1071 "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A.$\n\nThe solution is $y=A\\,\\cdot $\n{{field-1}}" 13194139538227 17592186576411 true] [17592186314785 1074 "To solve the homogeneous boundary value problem, we first find the general solution and then determine the values of the arbitrary constants that fit the boundary conditions.\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm \\text{i}.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-A\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n-A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=0\\,⇒\\,B=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition $y(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $A.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the BVP is $y=A\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ where $A$ is arbitrary." 13194139538227 17592186576411 false] [17592186314785 1074 "To solve the homogeneous boundary value problem, we first find the general solution and then determine the values of the arbitrary constants that fit the boundary conditions.\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm \\text{i}.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-A\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n-A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=0\\,⇒\\,B=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition $y\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $A.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the BVP is $y=A\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ where $A$ is arbitrary." 13194139538227 17592186576411 true] [17592186314786 1074 "![](images/24/24f07da42a537ded7916fc2440dcf3ee1ccdfc81c7b027b47e97814fec059f5a.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\frac{1}{2}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538227 17592186576411 false] [17592186314786 1074 "![](images/24/24f07da42a537ded7916fc2440dcf3ee1ccdfc81c7b027b47e97814fec059f5a.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\frac{1}{2}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538227 17592186576411 true] [17592186314787 1074 "![](images/68/68f1ebd0457e0c127f8ed6c979feba58196aa6bb43d97d211efa9b39bc33f637.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, substituting $x=\\sqrt{3}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}}sin\\,(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)) & =sin\\,(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =sin\\,(\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538227 17592186576411 false] [17592186314787 1074 "![](images/68/68f1ebd0457e0c127f8ed6c979feba58196aa6bb43d97d211efa9b39bc33f637.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, substituting $x=\\sqrt{3}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}}sin\\,(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)) & =sin\\,(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =sin\\,(\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =sin\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538227 17592186576411 true] [17592186314788 1074 "![](images/f5/f5cef3bda76e80edfa467a90823c9b6ad6aca1f8a5e8e3e06dc585258e975e0a.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty ,$ the graph tends to $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538227 17592186576411 false] [17592186314788 1074 "![](images/f5/f5cef3bda76e80edfa467a90823c9b6ad6aca1f8a5e8e3e06dc585258e975e0a.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty ,$ the graph tends to $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538227 17592186576411 true] [17592186314792 1074 "A first-order linear system of differential equations with two dependent variables is a system of equations that takes the following standard form:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=a(t){x}_{1}(t)+b(t){x}_{2}(t)+f(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=c(t){x}_{1}(t)+d(t){x}_{2}(t)+g(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $a(t),$ $b(t),$ $c(t),$ $d(t),$ $f(t),$ and $g(t)$ are known functions, and\n\n- ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are unknown functions (dependent variables).\n\nWith this in mind, let's inspect each system:\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t)\\,{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{1}(t)+\\sqrt{3}\\,t\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t), &  & b(t)=1, &  & f(t)=0, \\\\ c(t)=2, &  & d(t)=0, &  & g(t)=\\sqrt{3}\\,t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=cos\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}){x}_{1}(t)+{13}^{2}{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=9{x}_{1}(t)-\\pi {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is a first-order linear system of ODEs since it can be written in the standard form, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t)=cos\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}), &  & b(t)={13}^{2}, &  & f(t)=0, \\\\ c(t)=9, &  & d(t)=-\\pi , &  & g(t)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=2{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}^{2}(t)+{x}_{2}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is not a first-order linear system of ODEs since it contains the terms ${x}_{1}^{2}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\left\\{\\begin{al