mined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}t}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation for $y(t):$\n\n$$\n0-5\\alpha +6(\\alpha t+\\beta )=12t\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n6\\alpha t+(-5\\alpha +6\\beta )=12t.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6\\alpha &=12\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of }t) \\\\ -5\\alpha +6\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHence, $\\alpha =2$ and $\\beta =\\frac{5}{3},$ and so the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2t+\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, returning to the variable $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$" 13194139538227 17592186576411 false] [17592186335766 1074 "This Cauchy-Euler equation has non-polynomial forcing, but we are not given two fundamental solutions, so we cannot use variation of parameters.\n\nHowever, if we make a substitution $x={e}^{t},$ we can transform the Cauchy-Euler equation into a linear ODE with constant coefficients.\n\nLetting $x={e}^{t},$ we note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}=x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule to compute the first and second derivatives. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}, \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\tex