 answer. Therefore, our final answer is $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}.$" 13194139538227 17592186576411 false] [17592186335778 1074 "Since the point $A$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{2}{5}$ into the above and solving for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{25}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1-\\frac{4}{25} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{21}{25} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{\\frac{21}{25}} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the first quadrant, $y$ must be positive, so we select the positive answer. Therefore, our final answer is $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}.$" 13194139538227 17592186576411 true] [17592186335782 1074 "To find $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})+2x\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}))\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})-4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538227 17592186576411 false] [17592186335782 1074 "To find $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})+2x\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}))\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})-4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538227 17592186576411 true] [17592186335783 1074 "![](images/48/488ff32187c9a9a335e3782fb717242