538227 17592186576411 false] [17592186348271 1074 "The surface area of the given rectangular solid is $S=2{a}^{2}+4ah.$ Let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}S(a,h) & =\\left(\\frac{∂S}{∂a}\\right)\\,\\text{d}a+\\left(\\frac{∂S}{∂h}\\right)\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =(4a+4h)\\,\\text{d}a+4a\\,\\text{d}h\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $a=3$ and $h=7,$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}S(3,7) & =(4\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 7)\\,\\text{d}a+4\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =40\\,\\text{d}a+12\\,\\text{d}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538227 17592186576411 true] [17592186348276 1074 "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to write $(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}$ in the standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2y+{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is in standard form with  $P(x)=-2$ and $Q(x)={x}^{2}.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs." 13194139538227 17592186576411 false] [17592186348276 1074 "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to write $\\left(\\frac{1}{y}\\right)\\