\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =3-2\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =5\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $5\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139538227 17592186576411 false] [17592186351355 1074 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+C \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=3.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+C \\\\ 3 & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+C \\\\ 3 & =-2\\cdot 0+C \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=3-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =3-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =3-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =3-2\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =5\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $5\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139538227 17592186576411 true] [17592186351357 1071 "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ where $F(x,y)=(12{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{y})\\,i+(\\frac{x-1}{{y}^{2}})\\,j.$" 13194139538227 17592186576411 false] [17592186351357 1071 "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ where $F(x,y)=(12{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{y})\\,i+\\left(\\frac{x-1}{{y}^{2}}\\right)\\,j.$" 13194139538227 17592186576411 true] [17592186351382 1074 "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and  $x=-\\,\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}}$ is a root, its conjugate  $x=\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}})(x+\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{(\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{36}{5}(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{36}{5} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $5.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5({x}^{2}+\\frac{36}{5}) & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+36 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538227 17592186576411 false] [17592186351382 1074 "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and  $x=-\\,\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}}$ is a root, its conjugate  $x=\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}})(x+\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{\\left(\\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}}\\right)}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{36}{5}(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{36}{5} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\frac{6\\text{i}}{\\sqrt{5}},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $5.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5({x}^{2}+\\frac{36}{5}) & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+36 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139538227 17592186576411 true] [17592186351390 1074 "The rotation matrix ${R}_{\\theta }$ represents a rotation of angle $\\theta $ counterclockwise about the origin and is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorn