 compute $f(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}f(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{tx+3ty}{(tx)^{2}(ty)^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{tx}{2ty}) \\\\  & =\\frac{t(x+3y)}{{t}^{4}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{t}{{t}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{x+3y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{x+3y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}) \\\\  & ={t}^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{x+3y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}) \\\\  & ={t}^{-3}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)={t}^{-3}f(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y)$ is $-3.$" 13194139538227 17592186576411 false] [17592186357457 1074 "A function $f(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)={t}^{n}f(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $f(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}f(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{tx+3ty}{(tx)^{2}(ty)^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{tx}{2ty}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{t(x+3y)}{{t}^{4}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2y}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{t}{{t}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{x+3y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2y}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{x+3y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2y}\\right) \\\\  & ={t}^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{x+3y}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2y}\\right) \\\\  & ={t}^{-3}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)={t}^{-3}f(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y)$ is $-3.$" 13194139538227 17592186576411 true] [17592186357458 1071 "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)=\\frac{4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3y}{x}).$" 13194139538227 17592186576411 false] [17592186357458 1071 "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)=\\frac{4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3y}{x}\\right).$" 13194139538227 17592186576411 true] [17592186357458 1074 "A function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{n}g(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}g(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(tx,ty) & =\\frac{4(tx)^{2}-(ty)^{2}}{(tx)}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3ty}{tx}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4{t}^{2}{x}^{2}-{t}^{2}{y}^{2}}{tx}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3ty}{tx}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}(4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}{tx}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{t}\\cdot \\frac{4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3y}{x}) \\\\  & =t\\cdot \\frac{4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{3y}{x}) \\\\  & =tg(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)=tg(x,y)={t}^{1}g(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)$ is $1.$" 13194139538227 17592186576411 false] [17592186357458 1074 "A function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)={t}^{n}g(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}g(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(tx,ty) & =\\frac{4(tx)^{2}-(ty)^{2}}{(tx)}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3ty}{tx}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4{t}^{2}{x}^{2}-{t}^{2}{y}^{2}}{tx}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3ty}{tx}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}(4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})}{tx}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3y}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{t}\\cdot \\frac{4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3y}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =t\\cdot \\frac{4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3y}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =tg(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty)=tg(x,y)={t}^{1}g(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so t